## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates end-to-end diagnosis and mitigation of TLS handshake tail latency in multi-tenant Linux environments under production-like load conditions.

## Key Concepts
- TLS performance optimization, session resumption, and CPU pinning
- Hardware crypto offload and the kernel crypto API
- eBPF observability with per-tenant accounting
- Canary rollouts, staged rollouts, and rollback procedures

## Code Example
```bash
# Trace TLS handshake events with BPF (conceptual)
bpftrace -e 'tracepoint:tls:handshake {
  @tenant_id = comm;
  @latency = hist(nsecs - args->start_time);
}'
```

## Why This Is Asked

Real-time network paths require precise tail latency insight without burdening performance. eBPF offers low-overhead, per-event visibility ideal for diagnosing bursts in UDP data paths.

## Key Concepts

- eBPF tracing (bpftrace/bpfcc) for live latency data
- Tracepoints vs kprobes for kernel events
- Per-CPU histograms and BPF maps for aggregation
- Data-path isolation with tc and minimal instrumentation
- Safe rollback/guardrails if overhead grows

## Code Example

```javascript
function percentile(arr, p){
  if (!arr.length) return 0;
  const sorted = [...arr].sort((a,b)=>a-b);
  const idx = Math.ceil((sorted.length-1)*p/100);
  return sorted[idx];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this plan if TLS/DTLS terminates before the RX path?
